concepts.content:
Let's consider a point moving in a straight line in 3D with constant velocity over time: $\dot{\mathbf{P}} = (V_X, V_Y, V_Z)^\mathsf{T}$. At each time instant $t$, the point location is $\mathbf{P}(t) = (X+V_Xt, Y+V_Yt, Z+V_Zt)^\mathsf{T}$, and its 2D projection is:

$$\begin{aligned}
x(t) &= f \frac{X + V_X t}{Z + V_Z t}\\
y(t) &= f \frac{Y + V_Y t}{Z + V_Z t} 
\end{aligned}$$

If $\dot{Z}=0$, then the projected point moves with constant velocity over time. If $\dot{Z} \neq 0$, then as time goes to infinity, the point converges to a vanishing point:

$$\begin{aligned}
\lim_{t \to \infty} x(t)  &= f \frac{V_X}{V_Z} = x_{\infty}\\
\lim_{t \to \infty} y(t) &= f \frac{V_Y}{V_Z} = y_{\infty}
\end{aligned}$$

key_passage.text:
If $\dot{Z} \neq 0$, then, as time goes to infinity, the point will converge to a **vanishing point**: $$\begin{aligned}
\lim_{t \to \infty} x(t) &= f \frac{V_X}{V_Z} = x_{\infty}\\
\lim_{t \to \infty} y(t) &= f \frac{V_Y}{V_Z} = y_{\infty}
\end{aligned}$$

