concepts.content:
Assuming the scene is static and only the camera is moving, all observed motion in the image is due to camera motion.

If the camera moves in a straight line with velocity $\dot{\mathbf{T}} = \mathbf{V} = (V_X,V_Y,V_Z)^\mathsf{T}$, then after time $t$, the camera translation is $\mathbf{T} = \mathbf{V} t$. A point in space $\mathbf{P} = (X,Y,Z)^\mathsf{T}$ moves relative to the camera with velocity $\dot{\mathbf{P}} = -\mathbf{V}$. This moving camera scenario is equivalent to all scene points moving relative to the camera with velocity $-\mathbf{V}$.

Using the projection equation, the 2D motion field is:

$$\begin{aligned}
\begin{bmatrix}
\dot{x} \\
\dot{y}
\end{bmatrix}
= \frac{1}{Z}
\begin{bmatrix}
-f & 0 & x \\
0 & -f & y  
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
V_X\\
V_Y \\
V_Z 
\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}$$

The relationship for the 2D motion field due to camera translation can be expressed more explicitly by rearranging terms from the previous equation. This yields:

$$\begin{aligned}
\begin{bmatrix}
\dot{x} \\
\dot{y}
\end{bmatrix}
= -\frac{f}{Z}
\begin{bmatrix}
V_X\\
V_Y 
\end{bmatrix}
+ \frac{V_Z}{Z}
\begin{bmatrix}
x\\
y 
\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}$$

